<attribute name="refinedDescription" nodeId="e79c609">&lt;h3&gt;
    Roles and Responsibilities
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    This section of the staffing plan describes the roles on the project, including the boundaries and responsibilities
    within which team resources operate. Agile project teams are typically comprised of the following roles:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Stakeholders&lt;/strong&gt; - any person with an interest in the project that is not the Product Owner, Scrum
        Master or Development Team. This could be an end-user of the product, the sponsor, subject matter experts or
        members of other departments.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Scrum Team&lt;/strong&gt; - those directly involved with the project broken into the following roles: 
        &lt;ul&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;
                Product Owner
            &lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;
                Scrum Master
            &lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;
                Development Team
            &lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Agile teams have defined roles that are treated more as a boundary since teams are staffed cross-functionally. The
    Development Team consists of members having all the expertise and competencies needed to deliver without material help
    from outside the team and are self-organized to manage their own efforts in achieving the outlined goals.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    For more information on the responsibilities and characteristics of the Scrum team please refer to the &lt;a
    class=&quot;elementLink&quot; href=&quot;./../../practice.mgmt.proj.agile_pm.base/roles/product_owner_1F59E3A6.html&quot;
    guid=&quot;_8L_9oLAXEeeR2YAZ8achWA&quot;&gt;Product Owner&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a class=&quot;elementLink&quot;
    href=&quot;./../../practice.mgmt.proj.agile_pm.base/roles/scrum_master_F77379F2.html&quot; guid=&quot;_CGK2ALAYEeeR2YAZ8achWA&quot;&gt;Scrum
    Master&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a class=&quot;elementLink&quot; href=&quot;./../../practice.mgmt.proj.agile_pm.base/roles/dev_team_1F86862E.html&quot;
    guid=&quot;_xSPl0LAXEeeR2YAZ8achWA&quot;&gt;Development Team&lt;/a&gt; roles directly.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;
    Reporting Relationships
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    As there is no hierarchy between any of these roles (Product Owner, Scrum Master, Development Team), there are no
    direct reporting relationships in the traditional sense. Rather, Agile dictates that the team operates in a
    collaborative fashion&amp;nbsp;to provide a shared vision and optimize their teamwork in order to deliver the outlined
    goals as efficiently as possible. The Scrum team members plan, develop, deliver and improve together.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Scrum ceremonies such as the daily Scrum, sprint planning, sprint retrospective and sprint review are put in place
    to&amp;nbsp;enable this relationship of&amp;nbsp;information sharing and collaboration among team members. These meetings
    enable all of the&amp;nbsp;roles to be effective without relying on standard reporting relationships.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;
    Staff Management
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Stable Scrum teams are important in order to maximize efficiency and nuture the maturity of team relationships. The
    Scrum team should be involved in any staffing decisions as the team is supposed to be self-sufficient. The key piece of
    staff management is making sure the Scrum Master facilitates and coaches all members of the team on Agile principles so
    they can avoid impediments that would prevent progress.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;
    Onboarding and Release Timing
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    This section of the staffing plan describes order and timing of assembling teams and/or when each team member will be
    included in the initiative. Typically, entire teams are assembled at one time&amp;nbsp;to provide stability and reduce the
    amount of time spent ramping up or down.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Usually this includes description and timing of onboarding activities at the start of the project and handover
    activities at the conclusion during release.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
